  "title": "The fragment rule loops over every registered credential for each large number, making event redaction 16x slower",
  "detail": "The issue text was wrong here, sorry. It said numbers that are fragments of a credential follow 'the same rule that applies to strings', but strings are only redacted when they contain a registered credential, never for being part of one. The `for (const secret of credentialValues) if (secret.includes(text))` loop runs for every number of eight or more digits, and events are full of them (millisecond timestamps, token counts). Measured at production size: 20,000 events, each with seven such numbers, at 600 registered credentials, took 1,196 ms (59.8 \u00b5s per event) against 76 ms (3.8 \u00b5s) on main. It also brings back the per-credential loop that #706/#708 removed. Required: remove the fragment rule and keep only `redactCredentials(String(v)) !== String(v)` \u2192 `\"[redacted]\"`, which uses the shared matcher. Remove or adjust the fragment test, keep the tests for a credential as a whole number (nested and in arrays), for unrelated numbers unchanged, and for a too-large all-digit credential redacted as a string. Report one before-and-after timing at the size above in the PR. Run only test/credential-redaction.test.ts, `bunx biome check` on the changed files and `bunx tsc --noEmit`."
 }
]
